{% icon id="caravan" size=32 /%}Keep every front supplied. The Troop transfer {% term id="module" %}module{% /term %} keeps troops and {% term id="tool" %}tools{% /term %} stocked in your {% term id="kingdom" %}kingdoms{% /term %}. When a kingdom's castle holds fewer than you set, another kingdom's castle sends enough to fill it back up.

{% terms_on_page /%}

It is not an attack {% term id="plugin" %}plugin{% /term %}, so it does not count toward your {% term id="attack-cap" %}attack cap{% /term %}.

## How it works {% #how-it-works %}

1. A few minutes after your bot signs in, it reads the troops and tools of your kingdom castles.
2. For each rule, it counts what the kingdom to fill holds: units at home, out on marches, in production, and a transfer already on its way there.
3. When that count is under **When fewer than**, it plans a transfer that brings the kingdom up to **Fill up to**.
4. It sends only when the sending castle holds the whole top-up above **Sender keeps**. It never sends part of one.
5. All the units one kingdom needs go in a single transfer.
6. It checks again about every 30 minutes, or soon after a transfer that was on its way lands.

Only kingdom castles send and take transfers: the {% term id="main-castle" %}main castle{% /term %} in the {% term id="great-empire" %}Great Empire{% /term %}, and the kingdom castle in Burning Sands, Everwinter Glacier, Fire Peaks and the {% term id="storm-islands" %}Storm Islands{% /term %}.

## Write your rules {% #rules %}

**Troops to keep stocked** and **Tools to keep stocked** are tables. Choose **Add row** and fill in every column:

- **Kingdom to fill** is the kingdom that takes the units.
- **Sent from** is the kingdom whose castle sends them.
- **Troop** or **Tool** is what to keep stocked.
- **When fewer than** is the stock that starts a transfer.
- **Fill up to** is the stock the transfer brings the kingdom to. It must be at least **When fewer than**.
- **Sender keeps** is how many the sending castle always keeps.

The game takes one transfer into a kingdom at a time. So each kingdom has one sender: the first rule for a kingdom names it, and a later rule that names another sender for that kingdom is not used.

{% aside type="caution" id="troop-transfer-berimond" %}
The kingdom lists also offer {% term id="berimond" %}Berimond{% /term %}. A rule into or out of Berimond, or from a kingdom to itself, is not used, and the status says a rule can't be used. Berimond gets its troops through its own event.
{% /aside %}

## Time skips {% #skips %}

With **Use time skips** on, when a transfer is still on its way into a kingdom that needs more, the bot spends {% term id="time-skip" %}time skips{% /term %} to land it so the next transfer can leave. Your account settings decide which skips it may spend. It is off by default.

## Test with Log only {% #log-only %}

Turn on **Log only: write the transfers to the log instead of sending them**. The bot plans each transfer and writes it to the {% term id="log" %}log{% /term %}, but sends nothing and spends no skips.

## What it never does {% #never %}

- It never spends {% term id="ruby" %}rubies{% /term %}.
- It never takes a sending castle below **Sender keeps**.
- It never sends a part of a top-up. If the sender is short, it waits.
- It never starts a transfer within 5 to 10 seconds of another army leaving, so two armies never start close together.

## Read the status {% #status %}

The {% ui key="client:module.status" /%} card tells you what the plugin did on its last check:

- **Every kingdom is at or above its floor.** Nothing to send.
- **Sent** a number of transfers with a number of troops and tools.
- **A transfer is still on its way into that kingdom.** The next one leaves once it lands.
- **The sending castle doesn't have enough** of a unit for the whole top-up. It resumes when it has.
- **A kingdom in your rules has no castle on this account**, or it isn't known yet.
- **A rule can't be used.** Check the rule's kingdoms and amounts.
- **No rules are set.** Add a row.

